      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and plays a central role in Earth’s physical and biological systems. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar character. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs heat without large temperature changes, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows water to form droplets and supports small organisms. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many ionic and polar substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Transparency** – allows light penetration, supporting aquatic photosynthesis. |\n| **Biological Importance** | • Constitutes ~70 % of the human body and most other organisms. <br>• Medium for metabolic reactions, nutrient transport, and waste removal. <br>• Regulates temperature through sweating and respiration. |\n| **Geological & Environmental Role** | • Drives the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Shapes landscapes via erosion, weathering, and sediment transport. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to wetlands. |\n| **Unique Chemical Behavior** | • **Hydrogen bonding** – gives water its high boiling point relative to other small molecules. <br>• **Self‑ionization** – produces H⁺ and OH⁻ ions, giving water a neutral pH of 7 at 25 °C. |\n| **Common Uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation. <br>• Industrial processes (cooling, solvents, chemical synthesis). <br>• Energy production (steam turbines, nuclear reactors). |\n\nIn short, water is a polar, tri‑atomic molecule that exists naturally in three states, possesses unique physical and chemical properties, and is indispensable for life, climate regulation, and countless human activities."
